# Extra arguments for olrd. Read by olrd.service; run `systemctl restart olrd`
# after editing, or let `olr daemon listen` do both for you.
#
# olrd always serves its control socket at /run/olr/olrd.sock, which is what the
# `olr` command talks to. It does NOT listen on the network unless told to, so
# the web UI is unreachable from another machine until you set this. That is
# deliberate: installing a router's control plane should not put an admin port
# on your LAN without you saying so.
#
# To open the web UI on every interface, port 8080:
#
#   sudo olr daemon listen 0.0.0.0:8080
#
# which writes the line below and restarts olrd. Then open http://<this box>:8080
# and you are in — there is no password. Opening the listener at all is the
# decision; having made it, anyone who can reach the address can configure this
# router. olrd says so in the journal every time it starts on a non-loopback
# address.
#
# To require a token instead, add --auth. olrd generates one on first start and
# you read it with `sudo cat /etc/open-linux-router/api-token`; the UI asks for
# it and remembers it:
#
#   OLRD_ARGS=--listen 0.0.0.0:8080 --auth
#
# To close it again:
#
#   sudo olr daemon listen --off
#
#OLRD_ARGS=--listen 0.0.0.0:8080
OLRD_ARGS=
